steps.body:
Five minutes on your typing site. Home row first, then the punctuation this week is built out of.

Type these three lines ten times each, without looking down:

#include <algorithm>
std::vector<int> v(n);
std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());

Then these two, five times each, because the generator wants them:

import random
print(random.randint(1, 1000))

Two things to notice while your fingers learn them. std::sort takes two things, the beginning and one past the end, and v.end() is deliberately not the last item; it is the spot after it, which is why sorting a whole vector reads the way it does. And the two languages are sitting on the same page again this week: the strict one writes the answer, the quick one writes the questions.
